+## Turn usage — issue #93
+
+- `afterModelResponse` fires once after the round loop and receives the sum of input, output and total tokens from every provider call in that user turn, including both tool rounds and the final answer ([validated by: reports the sum of all three provider calls after two tool rounds](../../src/orchestration/usage.acceptance.test.ts#L60)).
+- Missing round usage contributes nothing to the sum; a turn with no reported usage receives `undefined`, and a turn without tools retains its existing usage values ([validated by: sums reported rounds around missing usage and leaves wholly unreported turns undefined](../../src/orchestration/usage.acceptance.test.ts#L70)).
+
+Issue #93 chooses a summed usage argument, preserving the existing object shape for no-tool consumers. A per-round breakdown is rejected because provider-call inspection is not needed for the billing and budget total and would widen the public surface. The public hook signature must name the value as turn-total usage. The implementation owes a MINOR bump and a changelog entry explaining that existing consumers will record larger values for tool-heavy turns.
+
 ## Measured semantics — out of scope
 
-These two properties of `src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts` are recorded so the hook features that follow specify against real behaviour. This issue changes none of them; each is a separate, out-of-scope change owned by no issue in this epic.
+The following error behavior remains outside issue #93.
 
-- The `catch` gates `onError` on `error instanceof Error` ([chatOrchestrator.ts#L150](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts#L150)), so a rejection that is not an `Error` — a thrown string or object — bypasses `onError` entirely while still propagating to the caller.
-- `afterModelResponse` is called once per user message after the round loop exits ([chatOrchestrator.ts#L148](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.ts#L148)), and is passed the `lastUsage` local — the most recent round's usage, not a sum across tool rounds, so a deployer using the hook for billing or audit under-counts every turn that ran tools.
